using System.Diagnostics;

public static void Insert(Stack s, int x)
{
    int oldSize = s.Count;
    for (int i = 0; i < x; i++){
        int inp = Input();
        s.push(inp);
    }
    Debug.Assert(oldSize + x == s.Count); // @@@ Size of stack increases by x in the function
}
</code>
The variable "oldSize" helps to get the value of the stack size before the operation, then this value is compared with the increased size of the stack in the operation inside the for loop. The value of "x" has also been included in the assertion to check if the operation has effectively increased the size of the stack by the given amount only.

<code>
public static int Padd(int x, int y)
{
    int sum = x + y;
    // @@@ return value is greater than or equal to x and y 
    Debug.Assert(sum >= x && sum >= y);
    return sum;
}
</code>
Checking if the return value "sum" of this function is greater than or equal to both "x" and "y" with a debug assertion. Here, "sum" is the return value in this case.

<code>
public static int Search(Collection c, int val)
{
    int index = c.SearchFrom(c, val, 2);
    // @@@ index is either -1 or greater than or equal to 2
    Debug.Assert(index == -1 || index >= 2);
    return index;
}
</code>
The idea here is to check if "index" (-1 or non-negative integer) returned from the function Search is either -1 (meaning val not found in the Collection), or it's integer not smaller than 2.

