In the given code block, the assert statements are inserted at the places where there exists a "@@@" remark. The 'OLD(variable_name)' syntax is used to refer to the state of the variable before the operation is performed. 'RET' is used to refer to the return value of the function. In the given example, an Assert statement has been added to ensure if the size of the array increases by 1, then the size of the array should be the old size plus 1 and the length of the array should not change or it should decrease by 1. The size shouldn't be decreased if the operation is an addition operation. The return value of adding an element to the list should be 0 as per the specification of System.Collections.ArrayList.Add method.--------------------
Debug.Assert(OLD(((ArrayList)ReturnValue).Count > 0 && ((ArrayList)ReturnValue).Count == OLD(list.Count - 1))));
</code>

